{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 0 and 12","reason":"The missing y^2 term forces a factorization of the form (x+u)(x+3y+v). Comparing coefficients gives u+v=1, 3u=m, and uv=-m, so u(4-u)=0. Thus m=0 or 12; both give integer linear factors.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_031","cnm_146","cnm_034"],"id":"solution:compare-linear-factor-coefficients:4a9070850092","label":"Determine the possible factor structure","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-linear-factor-coefficients":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_224","cnm_031","cnm_146","cnm_034"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: -7007","reason":"Write f(x)=(x+t)g(x). Comparing x coefficients gives 10+t=100, so t=90; comparing x³ coefficients gives a+t=1, so a=-89. Consequently g(1)=1-89+1+10=-77 and f(1)=91(-77)=-7007.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shared-roots-coefficients","label":"Factor using the shared roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shared-roots-coefficients":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, -7007","reason":"Write f(x)=(x+t)g(x). Comparing coefficients of x gives 10+t=100, so t=90. Comparing coefficients of x^3 gives a+t=1, so a=-89. Therefore f(1)=91(1-89+1+10)=-7007.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"linear-factor-comparison","label":"A linear quotient and matching coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"linear-factor-comparison":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 15","reason":"The quotient must be x+1. Expanding gives x^4+4x^3+12x^2+12x+3, so p=2, q=3, r=3 and (p+q)r=15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"determine-quotient","label":"Determine the quotient and match coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"determine-quotient":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 4","reason":"Native mathematical derivation agrees with the recorded choice; no grading change proposed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_020"],"id":"solution:local-factor-sign-and-degree:137c3939391f","label":"Use nonnegativity to identify a polynomial square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"local-factor-sign-and-degree":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"-2, 4","reason":"The expansion is x^3+(a+2)x^2+(b+2a)x+2b. Thus a+2=0 and b+2a=0, giving a=-2 and b=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_146_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_031","cnm_032","cnm_034","cnm_042","cnm_144"],"id":"expand-and-match-coefficients","label":"Set the missing coefficients to zero","methodDirectNodeIds":{"expand-and-match-coefficients":["cnm_146","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_146","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_089","cnm_031","cnm_032","cnm_034","cnm_042","cnm_144"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, m=32/9, between 3 and 4.","reason":"F=x^2+(8/3)x+m/2=(x+4/3)^2+m/2-16/9. It is a linear square exactly when m/2=16/9, giving m=32/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148"],"id":"match-linear-square","label":"Match a linear square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-linear-square":["cnm_153","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_153","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"-3, -10","reason":"(x+2)(x-5)=x^2-5x+2x-10=x^2-3x-10, so p=-3 and q=-10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The equality is interpreted as a polynomial identity in x, the ordinary coefficient-matching exercise convention."],"questionId":"cnm_150_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_031","cnm_034"],"id":"expand-and-match-coefficients","label":"Expand and compare coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"expand-and-match-coefficients":["cnm_146","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_146","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_031","cnm_034"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, b = −2","reason":"Modulo x²−x−1, we have x² = x+1 and x³ = 2x+1. The remainder is (2a+b)x+(a+b+1), so 2a+b = 0 and a+b+1 = 0, yielding a = 1 and b = −2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_042"],"id":"match-linear-quotient","label":"Method 1: Expand the possible quotient","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-linear-quotient":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 9","reason":"The factorization is (x-k)(x+k+1), so n = k(k+1). The values k = 1 through 9 work because 9 × 10 ≤ 100 < 10 × 11, giving nine values.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_150","cnm_014"],"id":"match-integer-factor-coefficients","label":"Determine the possible integer factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-integer-factor-coefficients":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146","cnm_150","cnm_014"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, some even number","reason":"If (px+q)(rx+s)=21x^2+ax+21, then pr=qs=21, so all four coefficients are odd and a=ps+qr is even. The value 58 works via (3x+7)(7x+3), whereas a=0 does not.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_014","cnm_p158"],"id":"match-factor-coefficients","label":"Compare coefficients and parity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-factor-coefficients":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_p156"]},"nodeIds":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_p156","cnm_146","cnm_014","cnm_p158"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 9 and -7","reason":"The equation is 2x²+(1-k)x+8=0. Equal roots require (1-k)²-64=0, so 1-k=±8 and k=-7 or 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_219","cnm_075","cnm_040"],"id":"match-a-repeated-factor","label":"Match a repeated factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-a-repeated-factor":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_148","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_219","cnm_075","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1","reason":"The product becomes [(x-1)(x-2)]/[(x-2)(x-3)] times [(x-3)(x-4)]/[(x-1)(x-4)], which equals 1 on the original domain x≠1,2,3,4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-and-multiply-reciprocal","label":"Factor and multiply by the reciprocal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-multiply-reciprocal":["cnm_160","cnm_158","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_160","cnm_158","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, p=6 and q=25","reason":"Modulo x²+2x+5, x²=-2x-5 and x⁴=12x+5. The remainder is (12-2p)x+(5-5p+q), so p=6 and q=25. Indeed, (x²+2x+5)(x²-2x+5)=x⁴+6x²+25.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_042"],"id":"match-factor-coefficients","label":"Match the coefficients of the product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-factor-coefficients":["cnm_150","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146","cnm_034"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_150","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146","cnm_034","cnm_032","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 5 factors","reason":"The complete factorization is x(x-1)(x+1)(x^2+1)(x^4+1). The last quartic cannot split into monic integer quadratics: coefficient comparison would require an integer square to equal 2 or -2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-and-check-completeness","label":"Factor repeatedly and check the remaining factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-check-completeness":["cnm_154","cnm_151","cnm_152","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_154","cnm_151","cnm_152","math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_150","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, c = 5","reason":"Setting x = −2 gives 0 = 4−2c+6, so c = 5. Equivalently, expansion gives 2b = 6 and c = b+2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_145","cnm_034","cnm_040"],"id":"expand-and-match","label":"Expand and compare coefficients","methodDirectNodeIds":{"expand-and-match":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cnm_146","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:factorization-coefficient-comparison","cn